# Line endings are LF in the repository, everywhere.
#
# Without this file git falls back to each clone's core.autocrlf, which on
# Windows is `true`: text files are rewritten CRLF on checkout and normalised
# back on commit. That round trip is not stable for the generated SPA bundle.
# A `npm run build` that changed nothing reported nine modified chunks, and
# their content hashes were identical, which is the giveaway: the bytes vite
# hashed never changed. Nothing was wrong with what was committed either, since
# marking the directory below made the nine disappear without rewriting one of
# them. It was git's own normalisation on comparison, and it is invisible until
# it wastes an afternoon.
#
# That also blocks the thing worth having. CLAUDE.md says to rebuild and
# re-commit internal/webui/public whenever anything under ui/ changes, which is
# exactly the step a hurried commit forgets, and CI can catch it by rebuilding
# and diffing. It can only do that if a no-op build is a no-op diff.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The committed SPA bundle is generated: vite writes it, we commit it verbatim
# so a fresh checkout builds without npm. Git must not rewrite a single byte of
# it, so it is marked binary rather than merely LF — minified chunks carry
# escaped CR and high-bit characters that git's text heuristic reads
# inconsistently, which is how this started.
internal/webui/public/** -text
